{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And you shall give the congregation and their cattle drink.</b> From here we learn that the Holy One, blessed be He, had compassion for the property of Israel." "<b>And you shall speak to the rock before their eyes.</b> This rock is the rock at Horeb, and it is the well of Miriam, according to the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory. We find for it four names in the Torah: עין משפט (En Mishpat), צור (rock), סלע (flint-rock), and באר (well). עין משפט — as it is written in לך לך (Genesis 14): ״וישובו ויבאו אל עין משפט היא קדש״, and it was called thus because there Moses was judged. צור — as it is said (Exodus 17): ״הנני עומד לפניך שם על הצור בחורב״, and it is called צור on account of the Shekhinah that was revealed there; but here in Kadesh, where it was not revealed, it is called סלע, as it is said: ״ודברתם אל הסלע״. באר — as it is said (Numbers 21): ״היא הבאר אשר אמר ה׳ למשה״. Because it departed with the death of Miriam, it now returned in the merit of Moses; therefore the definite article was used for it, as it is said: ״ודברתם אל הסלע״." "<b>And know</b> that opinions proliferated regarding this passage, and the commentators of blessed memory disagreed as to what the sin of Moses was. The view of Rashi, of blessed memory, is that Moses our master, peace be upon him, was not commanded to strike but only to speak to the rock, that it should bring forth water in the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is said: ״ודברתם אל הסלע״. And the reason he was commanded to take the staff was not for striking — for we find that all the signs that Moses performed were done with the staff of Elohim in his hand, as it is written (Exodus 4): ״ואת המטה הזה תקח בידך אשר תעשה בו את האותות״. But the sin was in the word ״ויך״ — that he struck the rock and did not speak to it, as they were commanded: ״ודברתם אל הסלע״. And therefore the punishment fell upon both of them, for both agreed to the striking and did not speak; and in this they weakened the power of the miracle, for the rock was worthy of bringing forth water by the command of the Holy One, blessed be He, through speech alone. And had this been done, HaShem would have been sanctified, for they would have said: if this rock, which does not know and does not hear, fulfills the word of HaShem, how much more so should we. But when it was done through striking, there was an opening for the lacking in faith to say that it was a matter done by way of wisdom and stratagem and not by way of miracle. And concerning this He said: ״יען לא האמנתם בי להקדישני לעיני בני ישראל״ — for through speech alone they would have sanctified the Name of Heaven." "<b>And the view</b> of the Rambam, of blessed memory, is that the sin of Moses was in the word ״המורים״ — that he became angry with them in saying so, and when one comes to a state of anger, one comes to a state of sin. And the Holy One, blessed be He, was exacting with him, that a man as great as he should become angry before the congregation of the children of Israel in a place where anger was not fitting; and anything like this, in the case of such a man, constitutes a profanation of the Name, for all his actions were directed toward the success of this world and the world to come — so how could anger be seen upon him, when it is among the bad dispositions and arises only from an evil intention in the soul? And concerning this He said: ״מריתם את פי״ — for the children of Israel were great sages; the least woman among them was like Ezekiel son of Buzi. And when they saw that he became angry — and he, peace be upon him, had no shortcoming of character — they thought that the Holy One, blessed be He, was angry with them for asking for water, and had it not been so, Moses would not have been angry. But we find no anger on the part of the Holy One, blessed be He, in this matter — thus far his words, of blessed memory." "<b>And the view</b> of Rabbeinu Chananel is that the sin was in the word ״נוציא״ — for when they said ״נוציא לכם מים״ in the language of those speaking on their own behalf, and did not say ״יוציא״ (He shall bring forth), they attributed the greatness to themselves; and some of Israel thought that since the Holy One, blessed be He, was revealed upon the rock at Horeb but here was not revealed, Moses and Aaron did this by their own wisdom — that they brought forth water from the rock, and it was not from HaShem. And this is what He said: ״להקדישני״ — that I be sanctified before their eyes; and ״לא האמנתם בי״ shall be understood as going forth to the children of Israel." "<b>Behold.</b> The verses will admit some of these great interpretations of the Rabbis, but the substance of the pericah does not settle on the heart through any single one of them. For the commentary of Rashi z\"l would have been sound had the speech alone been the miracle, and not the striking; but surely the striking was as great a miracle as the speech — against the rock, all is equal. And since HaShem, may He be exalted, said to him, 'Take the staff,' it was not for naught but for striking. Also the words of the Rambam z\"l, who said of himself that he untied by means of this one knot from the perplexities of the Torah, would have been sound had we found anger in Moses, for 'Hear now, you rebels' was a reproof and not anger; and Aaron, who was also punished, never became angry, for his trait was the trait of peace. Also according to the commentary of our Rabbi Chananel z\"l, had the verse said 'from this rock we will bring forth' in straightforward fashion, they would have attributed the greatness to themselves, and the commentary would have been sound and clear; but he said, by way of wonder, 'Shall we bring forth water for you from this rock?' — only that the Holy One, blessed be He, will perform a wonder for you by way of miracle. Yet with all this, it is his interpretation that settles on the heart more than all the others." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, the sin was in the word 'times' (פעמים), and with this all the difficulties depart and all the knots are untied. And the explanation of the matter in its secret: the designated Name that descended on Mount Sinai is that which was revealed at Horev upon the rock (צור), which is the place of HaShem, the dwelling of the Mighty One of Jacob, as it is written (Exodus 33:21) הנה מקום אתי ונצבת על הצור, and likewise it is written concerning Horev (ibid. 17:6) הנני עומד לפניך שם על הצור בחורב, and He commanded him concerning the striking, as it says (ibid.) והכית בצור. And the explanation of הנני is הנה אני, for the measure of אני in many places is the designated Name. And since all Israel were seeing with their eyes the glory of YHWH, and knew thereby the Name, the designated Name, therefore he struck only once; but here, at the rock of Kadesh, He did not specify thus to him, nor was the glory of YHWH revealed to them there as it had been revealed there, but he was commanded to take the staff to strike the rock before their eyes. Therefore they both agreed to strike twice, corresponding to the rock (הצור) and the Name, for they thought that the Shekhinah was with them in anger and that it was necessary to draw the power and the will from the supernal blessing from within the innermost. Now they did not sin in their intention at all, but their sin was on account of the Name not being sanctified before the eyes of the children of Israel, and this is (Deuteronomy 3:26) ויתעבר ה' בי למענכם, and it is written (ibid. 1:37) גם בי התאנף ה' בגללכם. And Israel thought that perhaps, perish the thought, his strength had weakened, and this is the sin, for it was a deficiency of faith in Elohim; and it was fitting to have faith in Him. Hence He said לא האמנתם בי — you did not place faith in My Name — He is the Elohim who is called 'Name,' He is the God of Jacob who turns the rock into a pool of water, and this is the meaning of מריתם פי — He who is called in every place (Exodus 17) פי ה'. And thus they said in the Midrash (Lamentations 1:18) צדיק הוא ה' כי פיהו מריתי — His mouth and the mouth of His intermediary; and this is the parable of the intermediary and the architect that the Rabbis z\"l mentioned regarding the creation of the world. And in another place He mentioned (Deuteronomy 32:51) מעלתם בי, that is to say, you dealt treacherously with Me in My Name. Now the sin is explained and explicit in the verses, for through the fact that the verse charges them with these expressions — לא האמנתם בי, מריתם בי, מעלתם בי — we understand what this sin was for them: they did not sin except in what they caused. And thus they interpreted מעלתם בי — you caused that the Name was not sanctified. And this is indicated by the language of Moses in his supplication when he was entreating Him, as he said to Him (Deuteronomy 3:24) אשר מי אל בשמים ובארץ אשר יעשה כמעשיך וכגבורתך." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to ask: just as we find at the beginning of Moses' prophecy, at the bush, that signs were performed there suited to the attribute of judgment — the matter of the staff that became a serpent, and his hand becoming leprous, and the waters turning to blood — so the masters of truth said here, that at first the rock dripped blood; and this is what they interpreted (Psalms 78) הן הכה צור ויזובו מים, and זיבה is nothing but blood, as it is said (Leviticus 15) כי יזוב זוב דמה. And we do not find any allusion to this in the prophecy of Moses, but in the words of the Kabbalah, which are Prophets and Writings. David said הן הכה צור ויזובו מים; (Isaiah 48) מים מצור הזיל למו ויבקע צור ויזובו מים; and this is alluded to in the expression (Psalms 114) ההפכי הצור אגם מים, that is to say, from blood to water. And because they sinned concerning the rock, which is the earth (הארץ) mentioned in the verse of Genesis, therefore their punishment was that they would not enter the land — measure for measure." "<b>And shall give its waters.</b> It should have said ונתן מים, but had it said thus, it would have appeared that the waters were beneath the rock, like some rocks that have springs beneath their roots; therefore it says מימיו, to indicate that they issued from the body of the rock. Or the meaning of ונתן מימיו may be the waters decreed from the six days of Genesis, which the Rabbis z\"l already mentioned regarding Miriam's well among the ten things created at twilight. And because through the expression מימיו a person might entertain a doubt — perhaps the matter was natural, that its waters were stored beneath it — therefore it was necessary to say והוצאת להם מים מן הסלע, that is, from the body of the rock. And it also used the language of הוצאה, which is an expression used for something that is miraculous and beyond nature, as in (Genesis 15:7) אשר הוצאתיך מאור כשדים, (Exodus 20:2) אשר הוצאתיך מארץ מצרים. And concerning this same matter Isaiah of blessed memory said (Isaiah 48:21) מים מצור הזיל למו ויבקע צור ויזובו מים — after he mentioned מים מצור, lest you understand that they were stored beneath the rock, he added and said ויבקע צור. Or he may say ונתן מימיו — in it itself, that is, that the waters would trickle from it, within it and around it; and והוצאת להם מים — that rivers drawing forth would issue from it, for it is impossible for all Israel, the men and the women and the little ones, to drink from upon the body of the rock and also to water their flocks there, unless rivers and broad streams were drawn from there; and this is the language of the verse (Psalms 78:16) ויוציא נוזלים מסלע ויורד כנהרות מים — ויוציא נוזלים מסלע explains ונתן מימיו, and ויורד כנהרות מים explains והוצאת להם מים."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :register {:flags {6 ["God"]}}, :notes {6 "Citations of Deut. 3:26 and 3:37 normalized: he writes (דברים ג:כו) for 3:26 and (שם א) for 1:37."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:55.559445941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}